  "narrative": {
    "intro": "United Parcel Service recorded $59.3M in federal contract obligations in FY2027 to date, spread across 234 awards with an average award size of $254K. Obligations are down 59.1% year over year. Fiscal-year obligations peaked at $345M in FY24 and trended lower through FY26 at $145M before the current-year total of $59.3M. The Department of Veterans Affairs is the dominant buyer in FY2027 to date, obligating $55.6M. Other buying agencies include the Department of the Treasury with $2.7M and the Department of Commerce with $1000K, followed by smaller obligations from the Department of Homeland Security, the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau, and several other agencies. The FY2027 picture so far reflects a much smaller obligation run rate compared with the prior three fiscal years, with FY22 at $238M, FY23 at $230M, and FY24 at $345M. Contract-count and average-award-size metrics show activity concentrated in many modest awards rather than a few mega-awards in the current fiscal year.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are United Parcel Service obligations in FY2027 to date?",
        "body": "The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $55.6M, which represents the large majority of United Parcel Service obligations in FY2027 to date. United Parcel Service has 234 awards so far, and the average award size is $254K. Other agencies each account for much smaller obligations, with the next-largest buyer, the Department of the Treasury, obligating $2.7M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "How does FY2027 to date compare with prior fiscal years?",
        "body": "United Parcel Service obligations in FY2027 to date are $59.3M, down from $145M in FY26 and far below the FY24 peak of $345M. Obligations were $238M in FY22 and $230M in FY23, showing that the current fiscal-year total is a smaller share of recent annual totals. Obligations fell 59.1% year over year."
      }
    ]
